declare c public.monthly_ai_catalog%rowtype; objective jsonb; extra jsonb := '[{"n":51,"id":"v51","word":"cellular","text":"In \"Why Are They Called Cell Phones?\", <u>cellular</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["organized into connected coverage areas","limited to one large transmitter","connected only by underground wires","powered by microscopic organisms"],"key":0,"explanation":"Here cellular describes the network arrangement of smaller coverage cells.","source":"Topic vocabulary: verified telecommunications sources","passageTitle":"Why Are They Called Cell Phones?","passage":"The word cellular describes the way a mobile network is organized. Instead of treating a whole region as one radio service area, engineers divide coverage into smaller areas called cells. Base-station antennas transmit and receive radio signals for these areas. The name refers to this arrangement of cells, not to a biological cell inside the handset or simply to the existence of towers. Antennas can be installed on buildings as well as towers.\n\nThe cellular approach helps increase network capacity: the amount of communication the system can handle. Radio resources are limited, so networks must utilize them efficiently. Frequencies can be used again in different places, with careful planning and control of interference, the unwanted effect of competing signals. This reuse allows more people to communicate than would otherwise be possible with the same radio resources.\n\nCells do not have to be uniform in size or shape. Their coverage can vary with antenna settings, buildings, terrain and other conditions. The neat hexagons in diagrams are a model, not the exact boundaries of real radio signals. Coverage areas can overlap; a phone may receive signals from more than one cell.\n\nAs a user moves, the network can coordinate a change to another suitable cell. This transfer is called a handover, or handoff. The network aims to keep the service working during the change. Cell sites are also connected to the wider network, which carries calls and data onward. Thus, a cell phone is named for the cellular network it uses, not for the route between two particular towers."},{"n":52,"id":"v52","word":"transmit","text":"In \"Why Are They Called Cell Phones?\", <u>transmit</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["send","weaken","store","translate"],"key":0,"explanation":"Antennas send radio signals as well as receive them.","source":"IMG_2352.JPG: transmit","passageTitle":"Why Are They Called Cell Phones?","passage":"The word cellular describes the way a mobile network is organized. Instead of treating a whole region as one radio service area, engineers divide coverage into smaller areas called cells. Base-station antennas transmit and receive radio signals for these areas. The name refers to this arrangement of cells, not to a biological cell inside the handset or simply to the existence of towers. Antennas can be installed on buildings as well as towers.\n\nThe cellular approach helps increase network capacity: the amount of communication the system can handle. Radio resources are limited, so networks must utilize them efficiently. Frequencies can be used again in different places, with careful planning and control of interference, the unwanted effect of competing signals. This reuse allows more people to communicate than would otherwise be possible with the same radio resources.\n\nCells do not have to be uniform in size or shape. Their coverage can vary with antenna settings, buildings, terrain and other conditions. The neat hexagons in diagrams are a model, not the exact boundaries of real radio signals. Coverage areas can overlap; a phone may receive signals from more than one cell.\n\nAs a user moves, the network can coordinate a change to another suitable cell. This transfer is called a handover, or handoff. The network aims to keep the service working during the change. Cell sites are also connected to the wider network, which carries calls and 