; a phone may receive signals from more than one cell.\n\nAs a user moves, the network can coordinate a change to another suitable cell. This transfer is called a handover, or handoff. The network aims to keep the service working during the change. Cell sites are also connected to the wider network, which carries calls and data onward. Thus, a cell phone is named for the cellular network it uses, not for the route between two particular towers."},{"n":60,"id":"v60","word":"handover","text":"In \"Why Are They Called Cell Phones?\", <u>handover</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["transfer of a connection to another cell","construction of a new antenna","replacement of a phone with a newer model","conversion of radio waves into stored energy"],"key":0,"explanation":"The passage explicitly calls the change to another cell a handover or handoff.","source":"Topic vocabulary: verified telecommunications sources","passageTitle":"Why Are They Called Cell Phones?","passage":"The word cellular describes the way a mobile network is organized. Instead of treating a whole region as one radio service area, engineers divide coverage into smaller areas called cells. Base-station antennas transmit and receive radio signals for these areas. The name refers to this arrangement of cells, not to a biological cell inside the handset or simply to the existence of towers. Antennas can be installed on buildings as well as towers.\n\nThe cellular approach helps increase network capacity: the amount of communication the system can handle. Radio resources are limited, so networks must utilize them efficiently. Frequencies can be used again in different places, with careful planning and control of interference, the unwanted effect of competing signals. This reuse allows more people to communicate than would otherwise be possible with the same radio resources.\n\nCells do not have to be uniform in size or shape. Their coverage can vary with antenna settings, buildings, terrain and other conditions. The neat hexagons in diagrams are a model, not the exact boundaries of real radio signals. Coverage areas can overlap; a phone may receive signals from more than one cell.\n\nAs a user moves, the network can coordinate a change to another suitable cell. This transfer is called a handover, or handoff. The network aims to keep the service working during the change. Cell sites are also connected to the wider network, which carries calls and data onward. Thus, a cell phone is named for the cellular network it uses, not for the route between two particular towers."},{"n":61,"id":"v61","word":"fixed","text":"In \"One Word, Several Meanings: Cell\", <u>fixed</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["unchanging","difficult to translate","correctly pronounced","scientifically measured"],"key":0,"explanation":"The opening contrasts a single unchanging meaning with several meanings.","source":"Studied vocabulary in a new context","passageTitle":"One Word, Several Meanings: Cell","passage":"An English word does not always have one fixed meaning. Consider cell. In a prison, a cell is a small room in which a prisoner is kept. In biology, a cell is a basic structural and functional unit of life. These two uses do not describe the same kind of object, although each suggests a separate unit or bounded space.\n\nIn an electrical device, an electrochemical cell produces electrical energy through chemical reactions. A battery can contain one or more such units. In a spreadsheet, a cell is one box where a user can enter data or a formula. Its position is identified by the intersection of a row and a column: in the usual letter-and-number notation, B3 is in column B, row 3.\n\nThese meanings are analogous in one respect: each identifies a unit within a larger arrangement. However, this comparison should not hide their differences. A living cell has biological processes; a spreadsheet box does not. The function of an electrical cell also differs from the purpose of a room in a prison.\n\nReaders therefore need to interpret the word in context. Nearby words such as microscope, guard, voltage or formula provide clues. A precise interpretation depends on those clues, rather than on automatically choosing the first dictionary definition that comes to mind."},{"n":62,"id":"v62","word":"cell","text":"In \"One Word, Several Meanings: Cell\", <u>cell</u> in the prison example is closest in meaning to:","options":["a unit that produces electricity","a small room for a prisoner","a box for entering spreadsheet data","a basic unit of a living organism"],"key":1,"explanation":"The question specifies the prison example, where cell refers to the room used to keep a prisoner.","source":"Verified meanings of cell","passageTitle":"One Word, Several Meanings: Cell","passage":"An English word does not always have one fixed meaning. Consider cell. In a prison, a cell is a small room in which a prisoner is kept. In biology, a cell is a basic structural and functional unit of life. These two uses do not describe the same kind of object, although each suggests a separate unit or bounded space.\n\nIn an electrical device, an electrochemical cell produces electrical energy through chemical reactions. A battery can contain one or more such units. In a spreadsheet, a cell is one box where a user can enter data or a formula. Its position is identified by the intersection of a row and a column: in the usual letter-and-number notation, B3 is in column B, row 3.\n\nThese meanings are analogous in one respect: each identifies a unit within a larger arrangement. However, this comparison should not hide their differences. A living cell has biological processes; a spreadsheet box does not. The function of an electrical cell also differs from the purpose of a room in a prison.\n\nReaders therefore need to interpret the word in context. Nearby words such as microscope, guard, voltage or formula provide clues. A precise interpretation depends on those clues, rather than on au