ather than on automatically choosing the first dictionary definition that comes to mind."},{"n":71,"id":"v71","word":"Perception","text":"In \"Perception and the Evidence We Notice\", <u>Perception</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["the ability to remember every detail","the act of producing a physical signal","the process of interpreting sensory information","the process of repairing damaged hearing"],"key":2,"explanation":"The opening explains that receiving signals must be followed by selection and interpretation.","source":"Shared perception reading theme","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when better evidence becomes available."},{"n":72,"id":"v72","word":"selective","text":"In \"Perception and the Evidence We Notice\", <u>selective</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["independent of the surrounding task","unable to receive any information","equally sensitive to every signal","focused on some things rather than all"],"key":3,"explanation":"The reader focuses on the paragraph while giving little attention to nearby speech.","source":"Shared perception reading theme","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when be