ferent outcomes.","source":"Shared reading and vocabulary material","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when better evidence becomes available."},{"n":77,"id":"v77","word":"bias","text":"In \"Perception and the Evidence We Notice\", <u>bias</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["complete absence of sensory input","systematic preference for one response","accidental failure of the equipment","accurate memory of an earlier event"],"key":1,"explanation":"The passage defines the bias as favoring an answer, such as saying yes when uncertain.","source":"Shared perception reading theme","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when better evidence becomes available."},{"n":78,"id":"v78","word":"specify","text":"In \"Perception and the Evidence We Notice\", <u>specify</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["explain in advance why something failed","leave undecided","state exactly","treat as unimportant"],"key":2,"explanation":"An explanation should state exactly what the demonstration measured.","source":"Shared vocabulary material","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when better evidence becomes available."},{"n":79,"id":"v79","word":"tentative","text":"In \"Perception and the Evidence We Notice\", <u>tentative</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["already proved beyond doubt","not yet certain or final","unrelated to the question","impossible to investigate"],"key":1,"explanation":"The conclusion has some support but does not settle every question.","source":"Shared vocabulary material","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when better evidence becomes available."},{"n":80,"id":"v80","word":"philosophy","text":"In \"Perception and the Evidence We Notice\", <u>philosophy</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["collection of measuring instruments","fixed sequence of laboratory movements","record of individual responses","set of guiding principles"],"key":3,"explanation":"Here research philosophy means the principles guiding how explanations are judged and revised.","source":"IMG_2348.JPG: philosophy (ticked)","passageTitle":"Perception and the Evidence We Notice","passage":"Perception involves more than receiving signals through the senses. A person must also select information and decide what it means. This process can be selective: a reader concentrating on a difficult paragraph may give little attention to a conversation nearby. The example concerns attention, not a claim that the reader has damaged hearing.\n\nImagine a classroom demonstration. Two listeners try to identify a faint tone among other sounds. Their prior experience may shape what they expect to hear. One listener reports a tone whenever the evidence is uncertain. The other waits for a clearer signal. The first listener may report more real tones, but may also report tones that were never played. A count of correct detections alone would not be a valid measure of which listener hears better.\n\nTo distinguish sensory ability from willingness to say yes, the class must consider both kinds of response. It should contrast successful detections with false alarms. This comparison reveals a possible response bias, or systematic tendency to favor one answer, rather than simply treating every confident reply as proof.\n\nThe demonstration illustrates why careful explanations must specify what was measured. A result may support a tentative conclusion without settling every question. An appropriate research philosophy is to compare explanations with evidence and revise an interpretation when better evidence becomes available."},{"n":81,"id":"v81","word":"fragments","text":"In \"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us\", <u>fragments</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["pieces of a larger object","instruments used to measure an object","layers forming inside an object","copies made from an original object"],"key":0,"explanation":"The passage discusses smaller pieces of space material.","source":"Shared materials and meteorite reading theme","passageTitle":"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us","passage":"Meteorites are fragments of material from space that survive the journey through the atmosphere and reach the ground. Many come from asteroids. Their composition can help researchers investigate their origin. A useful comparison considers the minerals in a sample alongside other evidence; outward resemblance alone is not enough.\n\nA meteorite can undergo changes after reaching Earth. Moisture and other conditions can alter its surface, producing weathering that damages some clues to its earlier history. Researchers therefore try to preserve samples carefully. The location and circumstances of a discovery are also relevant, because they help explain what may have happened to the specimen.\n\nNow consider a hypothetical collection. If one kind of fragment is easier to recognize, that kind may be found more often. A collection dominated by it would not necessarily be representative of all the material that originally arrived. It would be misleading to infer original proportions simply from the number of objects on display.\n\nResearchers must make any inference with these limits in mind. They can test an interpretation against chemical measurements and subsequent observations. An incomplete specimen can still provide valuable evidence, but a strong explanation considers what the evidence does not show as carefully as what it does."},{"n":82,"id":"v82","word":"composition","text":"In \"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us\", <u>composition</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["the place where something is found","the materials something is made of","the time something takes to move","the method used to display something"],"key":1,"explanation":"Minerals and other components reveal what a meteorite consists of.","source":"Shared materials and meteorite reading theme","passageTitle":"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us","passage":"Meteorites are fragments of material from space that survive the journey through the atmosphere and reach the ground. Many come from asteroids. Their composition can help researchers investigate their origin. A useful comparison considers the minerals in a sample alongside other evidence; outward resemblance alone is not enough.\n\nA meteorite can undergo changes after reaching Earth. Moisture and other conditions c