the same time","typical of the larger group","easy to identify by sight","more valuable than the rest"],"key":1,"explanation":"A sample biased toward easy-to-find objects may not reflect the wider group.","source":"Shared reading and vocabulary material","passageTitle":"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us","passage":"Meteorites are fragments of material from space that survive the journey through the atmosphere and reach the ground. Many come from asteroids. Their composition can help researchers investigate their origin. A useful comparison considers the minerals in a sample alongside other evidence; outward resemblance alone is not enough.\n\nA meteorite can undergo changes after reaching Earth. Moisture and other conditions can alter its surface, producing weathering that damages some clues to its earlier history. Researchers therefore try to preserve samples carefully. The location and circumstances of a discovery are also relevant, because they help explain what may have happened to the specimen.\n\nNow consider a hypothetical collection. If one kind of fragment is easier to recognize, that kind may be found more often. A collection dominated by it would not necessarily be representative of all the material that originally arrived. It would be misleading to infer original proportions simply from the number of objects on display.\n\nResearchers must make any inference with these limits in mind. They can test an interpretation against chemical measurements and subsequent observations. An incomplete specimen can still provide valuable evidence, but a strong explanation considers what the evidence does not show as carefully as what it does."},{"n":89,"id":"v89","word":"inference","text":"In \"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us\", <u>inference</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["description copied from a display label","mistake caused by poor storage","measurement recorded by an instrument","conclusion drawn from evidence"],"key":3,"explanation":"An inference is the interpretation researchers draw from the clues.","source":"Shared reading and vocabulary material","passageTitle":"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us","passage":"Meteorites are fragments of material from space that survive the journey through the atmosphere and reach the ground. Many come from asteroids. Their composition can help researchers investigate their origin. A useful comparison considers the minerals in a sample alongside other evidence; outward resemblance alone is not enough.\n\nA meteorite can undergo changes after reaching Earth. Moisture and other conditions can alter its surface, producing weathering that damages some clues to its earlier history. Researchers therefore try to preserve samples carefully. The location and circumstances of a discovery are also relevant, because they help explain what may have happened to the specimen.\n\nNow consider a hypothetical collection. If one kind of fragment is easier to recognize, that kind may be found more often. A collection dominated by it would not necessarily be representative of all the material that originally arrived. It would be misleading to infer original proportions simply from the number of objects on display.\n\nResearchers must make any inference with these limits in mind. They can test an interpretation against chemical measurements and subsequent observations. An incomplete specimen can still provide valuable evidence, but a strong explanation considers what the evidence does not show as carefully as what it does."},{"n":90,"id":"v90","word":"subsequent","text":"In \"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us\", <u>subsequent</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["independent","incomplete","later","contradictory"],"key":2,"explanation":"These observations occur after the earlier measurements or interpretation.","source":"Shared reading and vocabulary material","passageTitle":"What a Space Rock Can Tell Us","passage":"Meteorites are fragments of material from space that survive the journey through the atmosphere and reach the ground. Many come from asteroids. Their composition can help researchers investigate their origin. A useful comparison considers the minerals in a sample alongside other evidence; outward resemblance alone is not enough.\n\nA meteorite can undergo changes after reaching Earth. Moisture and other conditions can alter its surface, producing weathering that damages some clues to its earlier history. Researchers therefore try to preserve samples carefully. The location and circumstances of a discovery are also relevant, because they help explain what may have happened to the specimen.\n\nNow consider a hypothetical collection. If one kind of fragment is easier to recognize, that kind may be found more often. A collection dominated by it would not necessarily be representative of all the material that originally arrived. It would be misleading to infer original proportions simply from the number of objects on display.\n\nResearchers must make any inference with these limits in mind. They can test an interpretation against chemical measurements and subsequent observations. An incomplete specimen can still provide valuable evidence, but a strong explanation considers what the evidence does not show as carefully as what it does."},{"n":91,"id":"v91","word":"undertake","text":"In \"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes\", <u>undertake</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["describe without becoming involved in","provide money for someone else to do","begin and take responsibility for","bring to an immediate end"],"key":2,"explanation":"The historian plans to carry out and take responsibility for the study.","source":"IMG_2352.JPG: undertake","passageTitle":"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes","passage":"Consider a historian who plans to undertake a study of an old journey. Two travelers left accounts of the same route, but one describes business arrangements while the other records daily life. Each account offers a unique contribution: something not provided in quite the same way by the other. Neither should automatically be treated as a complete record.\n\nThe travelers may describe the same vehicle differently. A passenger interested in comfort might discuss the seats, while a merchant concentrates on the space for goods. Their purposes help explain which features receive emphasis. A historian should not presume that every difference is an error; people can notice different details without inventing them.\n\nHowever, a sensible explanation is not automatically a reliable one. Dates, maps and other records should be used to check the accounts. A preliminary comparison can reveal questions that deserve further investigation. If a writer could not predict a later event, a detailed description of that event may suggest that the account was revised afterward. This is a possible clue, not proof by itself.\n\nThe historian then examines a larger range of sources. Each has strengths and limitations, and a cautious revision of the first interpretation may be needed. Understanding a journey requires more than selecting the most entertaining story: it requires asking who wrote each account, for whom, when, and for what purpose."},{"n":92,"id":"v92","word":"unique","text":"In \"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes\", <u>unique</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["not provided by another in the same way","written much later than expected","more accurate than every alternative","suitable for every reader"],"key":0,"explanation":"Each account contributes a feature that the other does not supply in quite the same way.","source":"IMG_2352.JPG: unique (ticked)","passageTitle":"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes","passage":"Consider a historian who plans to undertake a study of an old journey. Two travelers left accounts of the same route, but one describes business arrangements while the other records daily life. Each account offers a unique contribution: something not provided in quite the same way by the other. Neither should automatically be treated as a complete record.\n\nThe travelers may describe the same vehicle differently. A passenger interested in comfort might discuss the seats, while a merchant concentrates on the space for goods. Their purposes help explain which features receive emphasis. A historian should not presume that every difference is an error; people can notice different details without inventing them.\n\nHowever, a sensible explanation is not automatically a reliable one. Dates, maps and other records should be used to check the accounts. A preliminary comparison can reveal questions that deserve further investigation. If a writer could not predict a later event, a detailed description of that event may suggest that the account was revised afterward. This is a possible clue, not proof by itself.\n\nThe historian then examines a larger range of sources. Each has strengths and limitations, and a cautious revision of the first interpretation may be needed. Understanding a journey requires more than selecting the most entertaining story: it requires asking who wrote each account, for whom, when, and for what purpose."},{"n":93,"id":"v93","word":"vehicle","text":"In \"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes\", <u>vehicle</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["place where travelers stay","means of transport","person responsible for guiding a group","container used to store records"],"key":1,"explanation":"Passengers and merchants describe features of the same means of transport.","source":"IMG_2352.JPG: vehicle (ticked)","passageTitle":"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes","passage":"Consider a historian who plans to undertake a study of an old journey. Two travelers left accounts of the same route, but one describes business arrangements while the other records daily life. Each account offers a unique contribution: something not provided in quite the same way by the other. Neither should automatically be treated as a complete record.\n\