ested in comfort might discuss the seats, while a merchant concentrates on the space for goods. Their purposes help explain which features receive emphasis. A historian should not presume that every difference is an error; people can notice different details without inventing them.\n\nHowever, a sensible explanation is not automatically a reliable one. Dates, maps and other records should be used to check the accounts. A preliminary comparison can reveal questions that deserve further investigation. If a writer could not predict a later event, a detailed description of that event may suggest that the account was revised afterward. This is a possible clue, not proof by itself.\n\nThe historian then examines a larger range of sources. Each has strengths and limitations, and a cautious revision of the first interpretation may be needed. Understanding a journey requires more than selecting the most entertaining story: it requires asking who wrote each account, for whom, when, and for what purpose."},{"n":96,"id":"v96","word":"reliable","text":"In \"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes\", <u>reliable</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["detailed in every respect","easy to distribute","worthy of trust","interesting to a wide audience"],"key":2,"explanation":"An explanation must be checked before it can be trusted, even if it sounds sensible.","source":"Shared vocabulary and reading material","passageTitle":"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes","passage":"Consider a historian who plans to undertake a study of an old journey. Two travelers left accounts of the same route, but one describes business arrangements while the other records daily life. Each account offers a unique contribution: something not provided in quite the same way by the other. Neither should automatically be treated as a complete record.\n\nThe travelers may describe the same vehicle differently. A passenger interested in comfort might discuss the seats, while a merchant concentrates on the space for goods. Their purposes help explain which features receive emphasis. A historian should not presume that every difference is an error; people can notice different details without inventing them.\n\nHowever, a sensible explanation is not automatically a reliable one. Dates, maps and other records should be used to check the accounts. A preliminary comparison can reveal questions that deserve further investigation. If a writer could not predict a later event, a detailed description of that event may suggest that the account was revised afterward. This is a possible clue, not proof by itself.\n\nThe historian then examines a larger range of sources. Each has strengths and limitations, and a cautious revision of the first interpretation may be needed. Understanding a journey requires more than selecting the most entertaining story: it requires asking who wrote each account, for whom, when, and for what purpose."},{"n":97,"id":"v97","word":"preliminary","text":"In \"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes\", <u>preliminary</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["based only on numerical data","unrelated to the main question","complete and conclusive","initial and preparatory"],"key":3,"explanation":"The first comparison prepares the way for further investigation.","source":"IMG_2348.JPG: preliminary","passageTitle":"Reading a Journey Through Different Eyes","passage":"Consider a historian who plans to undertake a study of an old journey. Two travelers left accounts of the same route, but one describes business arrangements while the other records daily life. Each account offers a unique contribution: something not provided in quite the same way by the other. Neither should automatically be treated as a complete record.\n\nThe travelers may describe the same vehicle differently. A passenger interested in comfort might discuss the seats, while a merchant concentrates on the space for goods. Their purposes help explain which features receive emphasis. A histor